1	//! Text Behind Subject at one instant (spec §11 CO-50, CO-51; §7.3): which
2	//! cut-out layers are behind each clip with `video.behind_subject`, the
3	//! SUBJECT COPIES that draw them into matte groups, and those groups merged
4	//! into the recipe's `matte` and `mattes` after the track groups.
5	//!
6	//! ### The relation
7	//!
8	//! A **subject** is a covering instance whose layer's `cutout` is non-null
9	//! and which is not drawn into a matte (`background` `remove` or `keep`
10	//! alike: a kept background still has a person in front of the room). A
11	//! **behind layer** is an instance not drawn into a matte and not an
12	//! adjustment clip whose evaluated `video.behind_subject` is true. Subject L
13	//! is behind B when, with d = B's nest depth, L is at least as deep, L's
14	//! first d enclosing nest clips are B's, and L's level-d track — its own
15	//! when L's depth is d, else its d-th enclosing nest clip's — has a lower
16	//! index than B's track in that container's tracks. So a subject inside a
17	//! nest behind a top-level title cuts it, and a top-level camera never cuts
18	//! a title inside a nest (the nest's composite is one picture to the
19	//! timeline; the title is not in it). No clip names another, so no edit
20	//! breaks the relation.
21	//!
22	//! ### The contract with the compositor
23	//!
24	//! A subject cutting at least one behind layer is LISTED. Listed subjects
25	//! take group ids n, n + 1, … in instance order, n one past the largest id
26	//! any layer's `matte` names (0 with none), so the track groups
27	//! (`recipe_matte.rs`) keep their ids and order. Each listed subject gets a
28	//! copy inserted directly after it — the original layer with exactly
29	//! `blend: "normal"`, `shadow: null`, `reflection: null`,
30	//! `cutout.background: "remove"`, `cutout.subject_copy: true` (appended) and
31	//! `matte: {into: s, cut: <the original's track-matte cut>}`, every other
32	//! key kept (CO-51, MT-4's third override) — whose Show Selection fact is
33	//! drawn-into. Each behind layer's `matte` gains its subjects' ids in `cut`,
34	//! ascending; each group `{group: s, mode: "alpha_inverted"}` is appended to
35	//! `mattes` after every track group. A frame with no behind layer cut by a
36	//! subject comes back exactly as it went in, so its bytes are what they
37	//! were before the flag existed.
